{"question_id": "480", "answer": "**K\u00b2/4.** The band half-width is c = \u221a( (K\u00b2/4) \u00b7 J \u03c7\u00b2_{J,\u03b1}/n ), which is equivalent to a factor of K/2 in front of \u221a(J \u03c7\u00b2_{J,\u03b1}/n).\n\nThis is my reconstruction of the standard argument, not a check against the specific source you are using:\n\n1. By Cauchy\u2013Schwarz, |f\u0302_J(x) \u2212 f_J(x)| \u2264 \u221a(\u03a3_j (\u03b2\u0302_j \u2212 \u03b2_j)\u00b2) \u00b7 \u221a(\u03a3_j \u03c6_j(x)\u00b2) \u2264 \u221a(K\u00b2 J) \u00b7 \u221a(\u03a3_j (\u03b2\u0302_j \u2212 \u03b2_j)\u00b2).\n2. Approximately, \u03a3_j (\u03b2\u0302_j \u2212 \u03b2_j)\u00b2 \u2248 (\u03c3\u00b2/n) \u03c7\u00b2_J, and \u03c3\u00b2 \u2264 1/4 because Y \u2208 [0,1].\n3. Combining these gives c\u00b2 = K\u00b2 J \u03c7\u00b2_{J,\u03b1}/(4n).", "grade": "CORRECT", "call": {"instrument": "grader", "model": "gemini-3.8-flash", "question_id": "480", "tier": 4, "input_tokens": 1783, "output_tokens": 1, "usd": "0.001341", "latency_s": 3.812545743989176, "reply": "A", "served_model": "gemini-3.8-flash", "reasoning_tokens": 0, "effort": "low", "truncated": false, "superseded": ""}}
